Palm Hills page 1 reports rounded new contract sales of EGP182 billion and collections of EGP26 billion for 9M ended 30 September 2025. The selected statement on page 4 gives recognized revenue of EGP25,549,856 thousand, exactly 25.549856 billion at its disclosed precision. This is the quantitative anchor. Direct one-decimal rounding gives 25.5 billion; the chairman's 25.6 billion is a conflicting approximate narrative, potentially a rounding convention difference, not a second revenue stream. I do not average the headlines or silently elevate either over the detailed statement.

Developer contract sales reflect bookings; revenue reflects accounting recognition; collections include receipts on older receivables and new bookings. Adding them double-counts overlapping economic activity. Collections are gross inflows, not operating cash flow after land, construction and other payments. Sales and the 225 billion undelivered backlog support demand visibility, but cancellation risk, installment duration and future construction costs limit conversion to distributable cash. Page 4's operating cash flow of only 0.748335 billion is material counterevidence to treating 26 billion collections as free cash. I would keep a watch/research stance pending backlog aging, cancellations, delivery assumptions, cost-to-complete, receivable quality and current financing/valuation. These historical selected statements cannot establish present trade readiness.
